        fp: "A commercial developer engaged a general contractor in writing to build an office complex for $2,000,000. Under the contract, monthly progress payments were due upon the issuance of an architect's certificate confirming that work conformed to plans. The contract provided that obtaining the certificate was an express condition precedent to payment. During the third month, after the contractor completed the foundation and steel framing in full compliance with all blueprints, the developer ordered the architect to withhold the certificate solely because the developer was experiencing an acute cash flow crisis on another project. The architect admitted privately to the contractor that the work was flawless, but stated: 'My hands are tied; the developer pays my fees, so I cannot issue the certificate.' The developer then refused to pay the contractor's $300,000 progress billing, pointing to the absence of the architect's certificate.",
